	(7, 1, '<p>Historical Cost Principle: This principle states that assets should be recorded at their original cost when initially acquired, and this cost should be maintained in the accounting records over time. For example, if a company purchases a piece of equipment for $10,000, it should be recorded at that amount and not adjusted for changes in market value.Matching Principle: The matching principle requires that expenses be recorded in the same period as the related revenues they helped generate. This principle ensures that the financial statements reflect the proper matching of costs with the revenue they helped generate. For example, if a company sells products in January, but incurs manufacturing costs in December, the costs should be recognized in the same period as the revenue generated in January.Matching Principle: The matching principle requires that expenses be recorded in the same period as the related revenues they helped generate. This principle ensures that the financial statements reflect the proper matching of costs with the revenue they helped generate. For example, if a company sells products in January, but incurs manufacturing costs in December, the costs should be recognized in the same period as the revenue generated in January.</p>', 0, 100, 390, 1, 1, '2023-07-10 05:35:58', '0', '2023-07-10 05:47:44', '0'),
	(8, 1, '<p>Historical Cost Principle: This principle states that assets should be recorded at their original cost when initially acquired, and this cost should be maintained in the accounting records over time. For example, if a company purchases a piece of equipment for $10,000, it should be recorded at that amount and not adjusted for changes in market value.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Matching Principle: The matching principle requires that expenses be recorded in the same period as the related revenues they helped generate. This principle ensures that the financial statements reflect the proper matching of costs with the revenue they helped generate. For example, if a company sells products in January, but incurs manufacturing costs in December, the costs should be recognized in the same period as the revenue generated in January.Historical Cost Principle: This principle states that assets should be recorded at their original cost when initially acquired, and this cost should be maintained in the accounting records over time. For example, if a company purchases a piece of equipment for $10,000, it should be recorded at that amount and not adjusted for changes in market value.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Matching Principle: The matching principle requires that expenses be recorded in the same period as the related revenues they helped generate. This principle ensures that the financial statements reflect the proper matching of costs with the revenue they helped generate. For example, if a company sells products in January, but incurs manufacturing costs in December, the costs should be recognized in the same period as the revenue generated in January.</p>', 0, 100, 392, 1, 1, '2023-07-11 06:48:18', '0', '2023-07-11 06:48:35', '0'),
	(9, 1, '<p>Historical Cost Principle: This principle states that assets should be recorded at their original cost when initially acquired, and this cost should be maintained in the accounting records over time. For example, if a company purchases a piece of equipment for $10,000, it should be recorded at that amount and not adjusted for changes in market value.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Matching Principle: The matching principle requires that expenses be recorded in the same period as the related revenues they helped generate. This principle ensures that the financial statements reflect the proper matching of costs with the revenue they helped generate. For example, if a company sells products in January, but incurs manufacturing costs in December, the costs should be recognized in the same period as the revenue generated in January.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Revenue Recognition Principle: According to this principle, revenue should be recognized when it is earned and realized or realizable. Revenue is considered earned when goods or services have been provided to customers, and it is realized or realizable when the company has the right to receive payment. This principle helps ensure that revenue is reported in the appropriate period.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Consistency Principle: The consistency principle states that once an accounting method or principle has been chosen, it should be consistently applied from one period to another. This principle enhances comparability and allows users of financial statements to make meaningful comparisons over time.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>Conservatism Principle: The conservatism principle suggests that when faced with uncertainty, accountants should err on the side of caution and choose the alternative that is less likely to overstate assets or income. This principle helps prevent over-optimistic reporting and ensures that financial statements are not overly inflated.</p>', 0, 100, 393, 1, 1, '2023-07-11 11:03:01', '0', '2023-07-11 11:03:08', '0'),
